lawyers representing the six cancer  patients walk to file their lawsuit at  tokyo district court they say tokyo  electric power company holdings should  pay more than five million dollars in  compensation after their clients were  exposed to radiation following the 2011  nuclear disaster in fukushima  one of the six plaintiffs who doesn't  want to be identified says cancer has  ruined her life  i always wanted to be a career woman  working hard in tokyo but i was  diagnosed with cancer and i still feel  ill after i had surgery since then i'm  in a situation where i have to  prioritize my health over everything  in march 2011 a magnitude 9 earthquake  struck japan's eastern coast it  triggered a tsunami that sent huge waves  surging as far as 10 kilometers inland  the fukushima nuclear power plant was  also overwhelmed and seawater flooded  its reactors  radiation leaked out forcing more than  150 000 people to flee  the six people suing the operator lived  in fukushima in 2011 and later developed  thyroid cancer lawyers say cases of the  disease in fukushima are significantly  higher than the national average  thyroid cancer is a rare disease that  only one in a million people get on  average as the number of children in  fukushima is around 30 000 only one  person would get it every few years  but after the nuclear disaster  266 people have developed cancer in  fukushima  a um report last year found the nuclear  disaster had not increased the risk of  cancer and the authorities in fukushima  say the high detection rate is due to  over-diagnosis  but lawyers say fukushima's owners  should be held accountable for radiation  exposures unless they can prove  otherwise  victoria gatenbee al jazeera
 

